# Whether an interval with 2-limb bounds is [0, 0] (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface", the
# row of certainly_zero()): the leading limbs of the bounds are zero, and a normalized pair whose
# leading limb is zero is zero. Exact. For every precision.
kernel:        interval_zero_2
input:         xl = xl0 xl1
input:         xu = xu0 xu1
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m eq x 0 x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the leading limbs of the bounds compared with 0 (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand
unused:        xl1 a canonical bound is zero exactly when its leading limb is
unused:        xu1 a canonical bound is zero exactly when its leading limb is

a = eq(xl0, 0)
b = eq(xu0, 0)
m = and(a, b)
